[{"doc_id": "doc_0605", "sent_idx": 20, "cleaned_version": 1, "sentence": "Once data is loaded it is possible to create tables where each row is a finished job, ordered by any column you choose — the @end_time timestamp is suggested — and any dashboards, graphs, or other analysis of interest."}, {"doc_id": "doc_37f2", "sent_idx": 8, "cleaned_version": 1, "sentence": "(lowercase "L") to view the following columns: FairShare, Level FS. Note that Level FS values are infinity if the association has no usage."}, {"doc_id": "doc_4387", "sent_idx": 93, "cleaned_version": 1, "sentence": "You can check the default collation for the database this way (a _ci suffix indicates a case-insensitive collation):
mysql> SELECT default_collation_name FROM information_schema.schemata WHERE schema_name='db_name';
You can identify the database tables and columns that do not have a case-insensitive collation this way:
mysql> SELECT table_name,column_name,collation_name FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_schema='db_name' AND collation_name NOT LIKE '%_ci';
